## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical UNIX text parsing, time bucketing, and cross-format handling with minimal tooling, plus robustness to unreadable files.

## Key Concepts
- Robust parsing of two log formats
- Time bucketing by hour
- Cross-file aggregation
- Handling unreadable files

## Code Example
```bash
find /var/log -type f -mtime -0.5 -name 'build*.log' -print0 | \
  xargs -0 awk '
    function hour(ts){ return substr(ts,1,13) ":00" }
    {
      if (match($0, /^([0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}) ([0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}) (ERROR|WARN)/, m)) {
        h = hour(m[1]" "m[2]); lvl = toupper(m[3]); c[h":"lvl]++
      } else if (match($0, /"ts":"([^"]+)",.*"lvl":"([^"]+)"/, j)) {
        t = j[1]; gsub(/[-:TZ]/, " ", t); h = substr(t,1,13) ":00"; lvl = toupper(j[2]); c[h":"lvl]++
      }
    }
    END{ for (k in c) print k, c[k] }
  ' \
  | sort
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for gzipped logs?
- How would you add unit tests for the parser?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to unify heterogeneous log formats, handle compressed data, and perform time-based validation in a tight, production-like scenario.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-format timestamp extraction (text and JSON)
- Decompression with zcat for .gz files
- Epoch conversion via date -d
- Sliding window filtering (60 minutes) and max absolute skew

## Code Example
```bash
find /logs -type f \( -name '*.log' -o -name '*.log.gz' \) -print0 | while IFS= read -d '' f; do
  case "$f" in
    *.gz) data=$(zcat "$f" 2>/dev/null || echo) ;;
    *) data=$(cat "$f" 2>/dev/null || echo) ;;
  esac
  echo "$data" | awk '
    /^[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2} [0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}/ {
      cmd = "date -d \"" $1 " " $2 "\" +%s 2>/dev/null"
      cmd | getline ts; close(cmd)
      if (ts > 0) print ts
    }
    /{.*"ts".*"lvl".*"msg".*}/ {
      match($0, /"ts":[[:space:]]*[0-9]+/)
      if (RSTART > 0) {
        ts = substr($0, RSTART + 5, RLENGTH - 5)
        print ts
      }
    }
  '
done | awk -v now="$(date +%s)" '
  {
    diff = now - $1
    if (diff <= 3600 && diff >= -3600) {
      abs_diff = (diff < 0) ? -diff : diff
      if (abs_diff > max) max = abs_diff
    }
  }
  END { print max }
'
```
